  "summary": "A style characterized by its distinctive mansard roofs and elaborate ornamentation, inspired by French architecture of Napoleon III's reign.",
  "description": "It features steeply pitched mansard roofs, decorative dormers, quoins, and often elaborate sculptural details. This style was popular in France during the mid-19th century and spread to America during the post-Civil War era, particularly for public buildings and grand residences.",
  "culturalContext": "Named for Napoleon III's Second Empire (1852-1870), the style spread through Baron Haussmann's Paris rebuilding and became the international face of progressive government: adopted for America's state capitols, county courthouses and Philadelphia City Hall, its mansard roof was a tax loophole made ornament (attic floors didn't count as stories). It communicated urbanity, imperial confidence and modern bureaucracy.",
